In a cabinet meeting convened to discuss plans for imposing martial law, Staal turned the tables against the chancellor by revealing the results of Commonwealth Army war games simulating conflict with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span>, <span style="color: #700000;">**Vanguard**</span>, and the Banners. The exercises concluded that the army would be unable to cope with a civil war against all three groups.

With his last hope for salvaging power extinguished and all but one minister turning against him, Goodryke is left with no viable path forward—resign now, or be crushed by the Assembly later in the week.

- @papen_2

@papen_2
title: Goodryke resigns his position!
subtitle: Who will succeed him?
on-display: {!  
    var cardEl = document.createElement('div');  
    cardEl.className = "face-figure2";  

    var image = new Image();  
    image.className = "face-img";  
    image.src = "img/schleicher_et_papen.jpg";  

    cardEl.appendChild(image);  
    var contentDiv = document.querySelector("#page #mid_panel #content");
    contentDiv.appendChild(cardEl);
!}

An embittered Goodryke—convinced that Staal orchestrated his downfall and vowing never to forgive the betrayal—has officially submitted his resignation to DuFour, who, with reluctance, has accepted it. 

As this marks yet another cabinet's fall under Staal's influence, DuFour's suspicions of his motives begin to grow, particularly with Staal’s constant invoking of national security as justification for his actions. And yet the President reaches for the man regardless. The gentleman Goodryke has failed; the parliamentarians failed long before him; if anything is to hold the Commonwealth together now, DuFour has decided, it must be the army—the last instrument of state that has not yet dissolved into faction. It is still for the people, he tells himself. Only now the people are to be saved by soldiers.

- @papen_3

@papen_3
title: General Werner Staal is appointed chancellor!
on-arrival: {!
Q.chancellor = "Staal";
if (Q.heartland_leader == "Goodryke") Q.heartland_leader = "Staal"
Q.staal_time = 0

Q.pro_republic += 5;
Q.army_militancy += 0.5;

Q.papen_goal = "Cry over spilt milk";
Q.staal_goal = "Scavenge for parliamentary backing";
Q.oskar_goal = "Be buddies with Staal";
Q.meissner_goal = "Become acquainted with the new chancellor";

Q.staal_papen_r = 0; 
Q.dufour_staal_r -= 1; 
Q.staal_strength -= 1;

!}
face-image: img/portraits/StaalKurt.jpg

Staal is now Goodryke’s successor, having orchestrated his downfall through manipulated war games conducted by his close friends, and has been appointed chancellor with full support from President DuFour.

However, Staal is deeply reluctant to accept the position. He prefers to exert influence through his extensive behind-the-scenes network, knowing full well that he lacks the public profile and charisma necessary to be an effective chancellor. Despite his reservations, Staal feels bound by duty to obey a direct order from the commander-in-chief.

His appointment is a breath of fresh air compared to Goodryke, and his more moderate and social leanings are more acceptable to the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> and the party system as a whole.

However, many still view Staal as little better than his predecessor. After all, he was responsible for the [? if otl_heartland_coup_seen:&nbsp;Heartland coup and the?] disastrous policies of the Goodryke government. At the end of the day, most folk view him as just another <span style="color: #808080;">reactionary</span>.